\title{A Sharp Order-Based Lower Bound for Adjacent-Vertex-Distinguishing Total
Colourings of Central Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study adjacent-vertex-distinguishing (AVD) total colourings of the central
graph $C(G)$ of a finite simple graph $G$. A total colouring assigns colours to
both vertices and edges so that incident and adjacent objects receive distinct
colours; it is adjacent-vertex-distinguishing when any two adjacent vertices have
distinct \emph{colour sets}, where the colour set of a vertex is the union of its
own colour and the colours of its incident edges. We prove that for every finite
simple graph $G$ possessing at least one pair of distinct non-adjacent vertices,
every AVD total colouring of $C(G)$ uses at least $|V(G)| + 1$ colours, so that
$\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge |V(G)| + 1$. The argument requires no regularity or
structural hypothesis beyond a non-adjacent pair. The decisive fact is that every
original vertex of $C(G)$ has degree $|V(G)| - 1$; consequently any two vertices
non-adjacent in $G$ form, in $C(G)$, an adjacent pair of equal degree, and such
pairs cannot be distinguished on a palette of size equal to their common degree
plus one. As a corollary we recover and strictly strengthen the classical bound
$\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge d + 3$ for $d$-regular non-complete graphs, and we sharpen
the five-cycle estimate from $\ge 5$ to the exact-order value $\ge 6$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Total colourings ask for a simultaneous proper colouring of the vertices and
edges of a graph such that any two incident or adjacent elements receive distinct
colours. The \emph{total chromatic number} $\chi''(H)$ is the least number of
colours in such a colouring. A refined variant, the \emph{adjacent-vertex-%
distinguishing total colouring}, additionally requires that adjacent vertices be
separated by their colour sets: neighbouring vertices must ``look different'' from
the local palette they see. The corresponding parameter is the AVD-total
chromatic number $\chi''_a(H)$.

The \emph{central graph} $C(G)$ is obtained from $G$ by subdividing every edge
exactly once and adding an edge between every pair of originally non-adjacent
vertices. Central graphs interleave a highly connected core of original vertices
with a sparse set of degree-two subdivision vertices. For $d$-regular
non-complete graphs, a lower bound of $d + 3$ for $\chi''_a(C(G))$ has been
recorded in the literature.

In this paper we identify the structural mechanism responsible for such bounds
and show that it is entirely independent of regularity. Our main theorem is an
order-based bound: $\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge |V(G)| + 1$ for every graph with a
non-adjacent pair. The regular bound $d + 3$ then emerges as a numerical
consequence via the elementary inequality $|V(G)| \ge d + 2$ for regular
non-complete graphs. The exposition is self-contained.

\section{Definitions and model}

Throughout, $G$ denotes a finite simple graph with vertex set $V$ and edge set
$E \subseteq \binom{V}{2}$; we write $|V|$ for the number of vertices and
$\deg_G(v)$ for the degree of a vertex $v$. We identify an edge with the
unordered pair of its endpoints.

\subsection{The total graph}

\begin{definition}[Total graph]
Let $H$ be a finite simple graph with vertex set $W$ and edge set $E(H)$. The
total graph $T(H)$ has vertex set $W \sqcup E(H)$, the disjoint union of the
vertices and edges of $H$. Two elements of $T(H)$ are adjacent precisely when:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $a, b \in W$ are adjacent in $H$;
  \item $a \in W$ and $e \in E(H)$ satisfy $a \in e$ (incidence); or
  \item $e, f \in E(H)$ are distinct and share a common endpoint.
\end{itemize}
This adjacency is symmetric and irreflexive, so $T(H)$ is a simple graph.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Total colouring]
A \emph{total colouring} of $H$ with colour set $\kappa$ is a proper vertex
colouring $C : W \sqcup E(H) \to \kappa$ of $T(H)$; that is, $C$ assigns distinct
colours to any two adjacent elements of $T(H)$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Colour sets and the AVD property}

\begin{definition}[Star and colour set]
For a vertex $w \in W$, its \emph{star} consists of $w$ together with all edges of
$H$ incident to $w$. The \emph{colour set} of $w$ under a total colouring $C$ is
\[
S_C(w) = \{ C(w) \} \cup \{ C(e) : e \in E(H),\ w \in e \}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[AVD total colouring]
A total colouring $C$ of $H$ is \emph{adjacent-vertex-distinguishing} (AVD) if
$S_C(a) \ne S_C(b)$ for every pair of adjacent vertices $a, b$ of $H$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[AVD-total chromatic number]
The \emph{AVD-total chromatic number} is
\[
\chi''_a(H) = \inf\{ n \in \mathbb{N} : H \text{ has an AVD total colouring with }
n \text{ colours} \},
\]
with $\chi''_a(H) = \infty$ if no AVD total colouring exists.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The central graph}

\begin{definition}[Central graph]
The \emph{central graph} $C(G)$ of $G$ has vertex set $V \sqcup E$, the original
vertices together with one \emph{subdivision vertex} per edge. Its adjacency is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $u, w \in V$ are adjacent iff $u \ne w$ and $u, w$ are \emph{non-adjacent}
    in $G$;
  \item $u \in V$ and $e \in E$ are adjacent iff $u \in e$;
  \item two subdivision vertices are never adjacent.
\end{itemize}
Equivalently, $C(G)$ subdivides every edge once and joins every pair of
originally non-adjacent vertices. We call $G$ \emph{non-complete} when it has a
pair of distinct non-adjacent vertices.
\end{definition}

\section{Structural lemmas}

Fix a finite simple graph $H$ and colour set $\kappa$.

\begin{lemma}[Star clique]
For every vertex $w$ of $H$, the star at $w$ is a clique in $T(H)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The vertex $w$ is adjacent in $T(H)$ to each incident edge by incidence, and any
two distinct edges incident to $w$ share the endpoint $w$, hence are adjacent.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Star size]
The star at $w$ has exactly $\deg_H(w) + 1$ elements.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The number of edges incident to $w$ is $\deg_H(w)$, and $w$ contributes one more.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Star injectivity]
For any total colouring $C$ of $H$, the restriction of $C$ to the star at $w$ is
injective.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The star is a clique of $T(H)$ and $C$ is a proper colouring of $T(H)$, so
distinct star elements receive distinct colours.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Full palette at a tight vertex]\label{lem:full}
If $|\kappa| = \deg_H(w) + 1$, then any total colouring $C$ of $H$ with colour set
$\kappa$ satisfies $S_C(w) = \kappa$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The star at $w$ has $\deg_H(w) + 1$ elements mapped injectively into $\kappa$;
since $|\kappa| = \deg_H(w) + 1$, the image is all of $\kappa$, and $S_C(w)$ is
precisely this image.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Adjacent equal-degree obstruction]\label{lem:obstruction}
Let $u, v$ be adjacent vertices of $H$ with $\deg_H(u) = \deg_H(v) =: \Delta$. If
$|\kappa| = \Delta + 1$, then $H$ admits no AVD total colouring with colour set
$\kappa$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $C$ be any total colouring with $|\kappa| = \Delta + 1$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:full}, $S_C(u) = \kappa = S_C(v)$. Since $u, v$ are adjacent, $C$
is not AVD.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Palette padding]\label{lem:padding}
If $H$ admits an AVD total colouring with $n$ colours and $n \le m$, then $H$
admits an AVD total colouring with $m$ colours.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Compose an AVD total colouring $C$ with the injection
$\iota : \{0,\dots,n-1\} \hookrightarrow \{0,\dots,m-1\}$. Injectivity preserves
properness. For each vertex $w$, $S_{\iota\circ C}(w) = \iota(S_C(w))$; since
$\iota$ is injective, distinct colour sets remain distinct, so $\iota \circ C$ is
AVD.
\end{proof}

\section{The degree of an original vertex in the central graph}

\begin{proposition}[Uniform core degree]\label{prop:degree}
For every original vertex $v$ of $C(G)$, $\deg_{C(G)}(v) = |V| - 1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The neighbours of $v$ in $C(G)$ are of two disjoint kinds: the original vertices
$w \ne v$ non-adjacent to $v$ in $G$, of which there are $(|V|-1) - \deg_G(v)$;
and the subdivision vertices $e$ with $v \in e$, i.e.\ the edges incident to $v$,
of which there are $\deg_G(v)$. Adding,
\[
\deg_{C(G)}(v) = \bigl[(|V|-1) - \deg_G(v)\bigr] + \deg_G(v) = |V| - 1. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:adj}
Two distinct original vertices $u, w$ are adjacent in $C(G)$ iff they are
non-adjacent in $G$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definition of $C(G)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Main results}

\begin{proposition}[Total lower bound]\label{prop:floor}
Any total colouring of $C(G)$ uses at least $|V|$ colours.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Pick an original vertex $a$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:degree} its degree in $C(G)$
is $|V| - 1$, so its star has $|V|$ elements, which a total colouring maps
injectively into the palette; hence the palette has at least $|V|$ colours.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No AVD colouring with exactly $|V|$ colours]\label{thm:exact}
If $G$ has distinct non-adjacent vertices $a, b$, then $C(G)$ admits no AVD total
colouring with exactly $|V|$ colours.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:adj}, $a, b$ are adjacent in $C(G)$; by
Proposition~\ref{prop:degree} they have equal degree $|V| - 1$. A palette of size
$|V| = (|V|-1) + 1$ is exactly the tight size in Lemma~\ref{lem:obstruction}
applied to the adjacent equal-degree pair $(a,b)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp order-based lower bound]\label{thm:main}
If $G$ has distinct non-adjacent vertices, then every AVD total colouring of
$C(G)$ uses at least $|V| + 1$ colours; equivalently,
$\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge |V| + 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $C(G)$ had an AVD total colouring with $n \le |V|$ colours, then padding
(Lemma~\ref{lem:padding}) would yield one with exactly $|V|$ colours,
contradicting Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}. Hence every AVD total colouring uses
$n \ge |V| + 1$ colours, and taking the infimum gives the bound on $\chi''_a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Order of a regular non-complete graph]\label{lem:order}
If $G$ is $d$-regular with distinct non-adjacent vertices $a, b$, then
$|V| \ge d + 2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The vertices $a$, $b$, and the $d$ neighbours of $a$ are pairwise distinct: $a \ne
b$; no neighbour of $a$ equals $a$; and $b$ is not a neighbour of $a$ since they
are non-adjacent. This gives $d + 2$ distinct vertices.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Regular bound]\label{thm:regular}
If $G$ is $d$-regular and not complete, then $\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge d + 3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Non-completeness supplies a non-adjacent pair, so Theorem~\ref{thm:main} gives
$\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge |V| + 1$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:order} gives $|V| \ge d + 2$;
therefore $\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge (d+2) + 1 = d + 3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The five-cycle]\label{cor:c5}
For the five-cycle $C_5$, every AVD total colouring of $C(C_5)$ uses at least $6$
colours.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$C_5$ is $2$-regular and non-complete with $|V| = 5$; vertices $0$ and $2$ are
distinct and non-adjacent, so Theorem~\ref{thm:main} gives
$\chi''_a(C(C_5)) \ge |V| + 1 = 6$.
\end{proof}

The naive regular estimate $d + 3 = 5$ is thus not tight for $C_5$; the
order-based bound sharpens it to the correct value $6$, demonstrating that $|V|$,
not $d$, is the governing parameter.

\section{Discussion}

In the central graph the interplay between adding non-edges and subdividing edges
cancels the degree information of $G$, leaving every original vertex at the
uniform degree $|V| - 1$. This converts \emph{every} non-edge of $G$ into an
adjacent equal-degree pair of $C(G)$, precisely the configuration the AVD
constraint cannot tolerate on a minimal palette. The bound is guarded by the
existence of a non-adjacent pair: for complete graphs $C(K_m)$ the obstruction
vanishes and the extremal behaviour is governed by the subdivision skeleton. The
extra ``$+1$'' beyond the proper total floor $|V|$ is exactly the slack needed for
adjacent colour sets to differ by a single omitted colour, suggesting
$|V| + 1$ is the exact value for all non-complete graphs.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Exactness for minimum-order regular graphs]
For a $d$-regular non-complete graph attaining the minimum order $|V| = d + 2$,
$\chi''_a(C(G)) = |V| + 1 = d + 3$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[The order term dominates]
For every non-complete finite simple graph $G$, $\chi''_a(C(G)) = |V| + 1$,
independently of the degree sequence of $G$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Complete graphs as the exceptional regime]
For $K_m$, the central graph $C(K_m)$ has no non-adjacent original pair, and
$\chi''_a(C(K_m))$ is strictly smaller than $|V| + 1$, governed by the
subdivision structure and growing as $\Theta(m)$ with a different leading
constant.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

For any finite simple graph $G$ with a non-adjacent pair, the central graph
satisfies $\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge |V(G)| + 1$, a bound requiring no regularity and
depending only on the order of $G$. The classical $d + 3$ bound follows as a
corollary, and the five-cycle bound sharpens from $\ge 5$ to the exact-order
$\ge 6$. The unifying insight is the uniform core degree $|V| - 1$ of central
graphs, which turns the AVD constraint into an equal-degree obstruction on every
non-edge of $G$.

\end{document}

